REVIEW RUBRIC (apply in this order):
1. The sk-doc template for the file type: references follow .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-reference-template.md, assets skill-asset-template.md, SKILL.md skill-md-template.md, ROUTER.md skill-router-template.md, README.md the sk-doc readme template (.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/assets/). Read the matching template before judging a file.
2. Render checks at zero: `JEV_FEATURES=0 python3 -B .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py <file> --json` lists render_* warnings (joined bold labels, setext headings, bare URLs, compound or empty overviews, repeated labels or sentences, letter-suffixed or unnumbered H2s, related resources not last, intro restating Purpose).
3. The intro after the H1 stays as written when it states facts (counts, ids, scope, decision references): never rewrite it. Only an empty intro or a pure pointer sentence is replaced, with a 1-2 sentence hook that does not restate the Purpose. A hook tells the reader when to open the file or what they get from it, in words the Purpose does not use. A pointer sentence ("The sections below cover...", "This reference explains...", "The examples below show...") is not a hook: never propose one, and flag one that exists. An intro that already works as a hook stays, even if it shares a few words with the Purpose. Before replacing an intro, confirm that every claim it makes also appears in the overview.
4. The overview opens with Purpose and When to Use.
5. A governing decision (a trust rule, a transport choice, a skip gate) comes before the steps it governs.
6. Sections and items sit in the order a reader uses them, not the order they were written.
7. A paragraph that packs several mechanisms becomes a list. Tables stay tables.
8. An index lists every file of the directory it indexes.
9. No progress tokens (TODO, WIP, "in this packet", "as of <date> in packet <id>") and no spec paths or phase or task numbers in the body. This rule is narrow. Decision ids such as ADR-001 are durable provenance and stay, with the decision they name. A decision record, changelog-style section or migration record exists to hold dated history: keep it, and keep every qualifier such as "historical", "superseded" or "deprecated", because removing one changes what the reader believes is current. Never move content into a new file. Source citations are provenance, not spec paths: a parenthetical such as "(research: <file> §N)", "(docs: <path>)" or "(snapshot: <path>)", and any statement of where cited paths resolve, stays verbatim with the claim it supports. Never change a citation's target, merge two citations or drop one.
10. Integer section numbers; a related-resources section, when present, comes last (before a trailing MACHINE ROUTER).
NEVER change what a rule, contract, instruction or example says. Text may move, split, merge or be reformatted; every claim must survive. Keep the whole frontmatter, rule ids, code blocks, JSON blocks, intent names, keywords and resource paths exactly: never edit a frontmatter value, the description included.

Inbound references are frozen. A heading listed under INBOUND REFERENCES keeps its exact text. A file listed there with numbered-section or line references keeps the order and numbers of its H2 sections: never move, insert, merge or renumber its H2s. Line references are rechecked by hand when the skill is committed. Reorder inside a section instead. Never propose a change that would break one of those references.
